To get Valkey actually running and serving your applications, you need four concrete pieces wired into your build scripts: package installation on the Reverse Proxy, firewall isolation on wg0, php-redis + CMS connection wiring on the Webservers, and password sharing between them.
Right now, ConfigureReverseProxy.sh configures valkey.conf if it is already installed, so here is the exact checklist to complete the loop end-to-end:

1. Install valkey-server on the Reverse Proxy

In your Reverse Proxy package-installation script, install valkey-server (with a fallback to redis-server for older Debian/Ubuntu LTS images):
sh
export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
if /usr/bin/apt-cache show valkey-server >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    /usr/bin/apt-get install -y -qq valkey-server
else
    /usr/bin/apt-get install -y -qq redis-server
fi
Use code with caution.

2. Firewall Port 6379 Strictly to Backend Webservers on wg0

Because your Reverse Proxy runs WireGuard (wg0) for both backend Webservers and potentially end-user VPN clients, lock TCP 6379 strictly to your backend webserver subnet (e.g., 10.100.0.0/24) so end-user WireGuard clients (10.100.1.0/16) cannot query or flush Valkey:
sh
# UFW example (adjust subnet to match your ADT server-mesh WireGuard range)
/usr/sbin/ufw allow in on wg0 from 10.100.0.0/24 to any port 6379 proto tcp
Use code with caution.

3. Share the Valkey Password & Proxy wg0 IP with Webservers

Your ConfigureReverseProxy.sh generates ${HOME}/runtime/valkey_password.dat. Push both that password and the Reverse Proxy's regional wg0 IP to your shared datastore/S3 bucket (alongside wherever you already store regional Reverse Proxy metadata) so Webservers in that region can read:
• REGIONAL_VALKEY_HOST (e.g., 10.100.0.1)
• REGIONAL_VALKEY_PASS (the generated token from valkey_password.dat)

4. Install php-redis & Wire Up the CMS on Webservers

On your Webserver nodes, install the PHP Redis extension (which speaks the exact same wire protocol as Valkey) and inject the connection settings into your CMS config during deployment:
• Package install (on Webserver):sh
/usr/bin/apt-get install -y -qq "php${PHP_VERSION}-redis"
Use code with caution.
• Joomla (configuration.php):php
public $cache_handler = 'redis';
public $session_handler = 'redis';
public $redis_server_host = '10.100.0.1';
public $redis_server_port = 6379;
public $redis_server_auth = 'YOUR_VALKEY_PASSWORD';
public $redis_server_db = 0;
Use code with caution.
